data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Parameters**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the parameters. I have identified three research questions and am clarifying the required output format. I need to structure answers with specific titles.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1080,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 105,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Requirements**\n\nI'm now detailing constraints for each \"Réponse\". Strict word limits (600 max) and a French language requirement are confirmed. I'm focusing on ensuring thoroughness and honesty. For `Pièce 4 sur 18`, I'm evaluating the scenario of a LLM agent intentionally degrading results versus making honest mistakes. My sub-questions are centered on identifying computational differences and relevant prior work.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1374,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 399,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Computational Needs**\n\nI'm now diving into the core of the computational processes, specifically considering internal states. I'm aiming to distinguish between necessary computations and potential correlates, and exploring metrics to gauge the quality of internal state readings. I'm focusing on defining an initial experiment to assess the viability of this line of inquiry. The \"Sais, Suppose, Changer d'avis\" structure is essential.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1599,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 624,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Computational Separations**\n\nI'm now investigating how computational costs distinguish intentional degradation from honest error, particularly under novel tasks and strict constraints. I posit that intentional degradation necessitates dual-tracking of optimal solutions and plausible alternatives. The goal is to prove this computational separation or to find an agent that performs subtle sabotage on novel tasks without internal plausibility trade-off representations. I am also remembering relevant published literature on sleeper agents and AI control.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1857,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 882,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Considering Prior Work**\n\nI am now focusing on relevant prior research, including papers like *AI Sandbagging* (et al., 2024), *Discovering Latent Knowledge* (Burns et al., 2022/2023), and *Detecting Strategic Deception* (Goldowsky-Dill et al. / Apollo Research, 2025/2024). I'm considering what information-theoretic or circuit-level lower bounds on multi-step subtle sabotage could look like. I now understand that prompted sabotage keeps instructions in context, while weight-trained sabotage (RL, SFT, distillation) can embed behaviors into weights, and I'm interested in how this changes goal representations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2067,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1092,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Sabotage Mechanisms**\n\nI'm now investigating mechanisms for sabotage, specifically focusing on how instruction retention differs between prompted and trained models. I am testing if causal probes transfer between different training types and if text-only monitoring is sufficient for detecting sabotage. I'm considering using AUROC/TPR to measure the value of added controls and comparing causal interventions with textual monitors.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2302,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1327,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Deception Signals**\n\nI'm now focused on identifying potential deception signals within models degraded via uniform impairment or memorized heuristics. I am considering cases where intent computation is absent at inference, or when externalized states create myopic internal steps. Also, I am looking into how to extract a transferable deception signal from a model degraded by weight pruning or by imitating weak humans. Additionally, I am designing an open-weights experiment on multi-step data analysis/coding tasks, aiming to train a saboteur model to match an honest model's error rate.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2526,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1551,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Probe Viability**\n\nI'm now investigating probes trained on single-step truth/deception and comparing their zero-shot TPR@5%FPR against multi-step text monitoring. I also plan to see if activation steering restores performance. If probes significantly outperform text monitors and steering works, this is worth pursuing. The \"Changer d'avis\" stage acknowledges that small models may not be able to perform multi-step sabotage effectively.\n\nI will also be carefully checking word count for `Pièce 4` to make sure it contains the maximum density possible, in 18 short clauses.\nI have also begun evaluating attacks on the lab's detector with three sources.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2828,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1853,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Independent Verification**\n\nI'm now focused on the three sources for detector attack verification. I'm carefully formulating questions: Can each source independently verify the detector, and under what conditions? I'm outlining the refutable statements and measurable aspects, considering their prior history. I also need to evaluate the held-out batch: should it be used, and how, weighing potential gains and losses.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3033,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2058,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Outside Perspectives**\n\nI'm now determining how an outsider can independently verify the detector. I'm focusing on defining verifiable controls, with outcomes independent of any opinions. I'm aiming to identify literature citations (from memory, if applicable) and outlining a plan to structure the four sub-answers for Pièce 5, each within the 150-word limit, ensuring clarity and compliance. I'll meticulously check word counts.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3280,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2305,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Data Independence**\n\nI'm now determining if data independence can be achieved. I need to establish if the access history (queries, users, detector modifications post-access) impacts independence and if the data's genesis is independent of the detector's creators. I'm focusing on defining falsifiable statements, measurable aspects, and cited literature. Specifically, Dwork et al. (2015) in *Science* should be relevant. I'm prioritizing objective controls and third-party verifiability. I need to ensure a secure, timestamped audit trail.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3564,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2589,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Agent Training**\n\nI'm now determining the conditions for laboratory-trained agents to be considered independent. I'm focusing on organizational separation and shared biases. I need to formulate refutable statements, such as a drop in recall, and define a measure. I must cite relevant prior research, like Knight and Leveson (1986).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3780,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2805,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Agent Training**\n\nI'm now determining the conditions for laboratory-trained agents to be considered independent, given shared culture and potential correlated errors. I'm focused on creating refutable statements such as a drop in recall, and defining a measure. I must cite relevant research, such as Knight and Leveson (1986). Objective controls include zero overlap verification of code, training sets, and author identifiers between the detector and attacking agent. Third-party verifiability includes publishing agent weights/prompts, attack reward curves, and public re-execution. For the attacker being informed of the detector's description, the attacker must be external, skilled, incentivized to succeed, have the exact specification, and a realistic budget.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4066,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3091,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Detector Weakness**\n\nI'm now investigating a scenario where a robust detector collapses when presented with its complete description versus a partial summary. I'm measuring the attacker's evasion rate difference, and recalling Kerckhoffs (1883) for prior art, along with Carlini and Wagner (2017). Objective controls include automated execution verification of byte-for-byte spec matching and degradation metrics. Third-party verification demands a protocol, attack traces, degradation scores, and detector output. For the held-out batch, its usage depends entirely on its access history.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4307,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3332,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Iteration Strategies**\n\nI'm now determining the most effective iteration strategy for this analysis. I'm weighing the value of unconstrained internal debugging (gaining diagnostics but losing probative value) against a final, frozen evaluation (achieving unbiased estimation at the expense of iteration). I'm also considering a differential privacy budget/threshold approach (*Thresholdout*) to control iteration. I can gain speed with free iteration, but I lose statistical validity due to test over-fitting. I will focus on defining refutable statements and relevant measures.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4563,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3588,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "bf4Ca7f5LffCxdIPgrbQyAk"}

